  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting the Protection of Internally Displaced Persons, Migrants and Host Communities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Overlapping crisis and conflict in Yemen for over six years threatens to push Yemen to the brink in 2021. According to the Yemen Humanitarian Response Plan (HRP) over 20.7 million people need some form of humanitarian and protection assistance. Of these some 4 million are facing protracted displacement or facing repeat and renewed displacement as hostilities continue risking loss of life, loss of basic means of survival, and exacerbation of protection risks as safety nets, while social support mechanisms are compromised. Through this project, IOM seeks to maintain and expand protection support with a focus on Marib, Aden, Sana&apos;a, as well as southern Hudaydah and southern Taizz. Specifically, IOM intends to provide protection support in Marib, southern Taiz and southern Hodeida where internally displaced persons&apos; needs and the potential for new and repeat displacements are high, while access to protection services is extremely limited. In addition to the widespread humanitarian needs evident across conflict-affected communities in Yemen, humanitarian needs are also prevalent among migrant communities. IOM is seeking resources to expand its migrant response programme to provide humanitarian and protection support to migrants, in the form of humanitarian assistance, screening and return support through IOM&apos;s Voluntary Humanitarian Returns programme.</narrative>
  </description>
